# The catalogue builder writes `catalogues/content_manifest.yaml` through `atomic_json`,
# which is a binary write, so the bytes it hashes into
# `catalogues/generated/catalogue-inputs.json` are LF-terminated. With `core.autocrlf`
# enabled, Git would otherwise materialise the file as CRLF on checkout: the content stays
# identical but its sha256 changes, and `:app:verifyCatalogueContent` then reports a stale
# catalogue and blocks every build. Pinning the checkout to LF keeps the file byte-identical
# to what the builder wrote and hashed.
#
# The same applies to `catalogues/achievements.yaml`, which stopped being a hand-edited
# source when the curation moved into the importer: `import_broad_catalogues.py` writes it
# as JSON, and the hash recorded for it in `catalogue-inputs.json` is that LF form. Without
# this pin it checks out as CRLF and fails the gate on Windows while passing on Linux.
catalogues/achievements.yaml text eol=lf
catalogues/content_manifest.yaml text eol=lf

# Every other hashed input is a hand-edited source whose recorded hash is its CRLF
# working-tree form; pinning those to LF would break the same gate in the opposite
# direction. Add a file here only when the builder, not a person, writes it.

# The generated catalogue is binary and must never be line-ending converted.
*.sqlite binary
